Do you think it's particularly more items (and monsters) than Crawl,
or do you think Crawl is just more willing to take up gobs of RAM
and disk space? (Running a public crawl server has certainly put
a greater load on my machine than I would expect from NetHack, but 
I haven't really though about the potentially different amounts of
items in-game.)

>      NetHack already maintains a pair of lists to handle monsters
> who are moving between levels (variety of reasons) and objects
> which are moving between levels (usually stuff dropped down holes)
> and every time you go to another level, it checks those lists to
> see whether anything needs to arrive.  Once the Wizard of Yendor
> is in play, if he is left alive when you leave whichever level
> he's on, he gets moved onto the migrating monsters list instead
> of remaining with the deactivated level.  That way he can be
> brought into play on your new level when he's needed.  And if
> you return to his level before that happens, he gets put back
> there as if nothing out of the ordinary had happened.  It would
> be feasible to do something similar for special objects.  But
> doing so just for end-of-game disclosure seems fairly pointless
> (no pun intended...) to me.

Oh, I 100% agree with that. The only reason I would want such a feature
would be for use during the course of the game, and I think it's more
appropriate in a game where the average win is 100000 turns than in a
game where the average win is 30000 turns. I would certainly find it 
useful, but I'm perfectly happy to play NetHack without it.

As far as special objects go, I know people who leave the Bell behind
and have to go search for it get very, very annoyed; however, I could
get behind the school of thought that they should have carried the thing
with them. :) 

